{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vmis1WqgxEU2npuP7SrLLr","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd002-sys\n     2→title: \"pkg/sys: Syscall Abstractions\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Four utilities (ls, du, find, xargs) need OS-level capabilities that Go's standard\n     6→  library does not expose in a portable, stable form: terminal-width queries, extended\n     7→  file metadata (hard-link count, device and inode numbers, physical block counts),\n     8→  and signal handling for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH. On Darwin and Linux these syscalls\n     9→  diverge in struct layout (st_mtimespec vs st_mtim, st_blocks interpretation) and\n    10→  available interfaces (TIOCGWINSZ ioctl). Without pkg/sys each cmd/ package would\n    11→  import syscall or golang.org/x/sys directly, duplicating the Darwin/Linux divergence\n    12→  handling and making it harder to test or audit the syscall surface.\n    13→\n    14→  pkg/sys provides a stable, tested interface for these three capability areas. Each\n    15→  capability is driven by at least one concrete utility requirement derived from C source\n    16→  analysis (see dependency-map.yaml).\n    17→\n    18→goals:\n    19→  - G1: Provide a portable terminal-width query and SIGPIPE handler that work on Darwin and Linux without cgo.\n    20→  - G2: Expose extended file metadata (st_nlink, st_uid, st_gid, st_rdev, st_blocks, st_ino, st_dev) not available from os.FileInfo.\n    21→  - G3: Provide a SIGWINCH handler that calls a user-supplied callback when the terminal is resized.\n    22→\n    23→requirements:\n    24→  R1:\n    25→    title: Terminal width and TTY detection\n    26→    items:\n    27→      - R1.1: Must provide func TerminalWidth() (int, error) that returns the current terminal column count by calling ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ) on stdout. Returns an error (not panic) when stdout is not a terminal or when ioctl fails.\n    28→      - R1.2: Must use golang.org/x/sys/unix (the project's only external dependency) to perform the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl; must not shell out to tput or stty and must not import golang.org/x/term.\n    29→      - R1.3: |\n    30→          Must provide func IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool that returns true when the file\n    31→          descriptor refers to a terminal, and false otherwise. Implementation: call\n    32→          golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize(int(fd), unix.TIOCGWINSZ) and return\n    33→          err == nil. Returns false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors.\n    34→          The caller (pkg/format ColorEnabled) is responsible for extracting the fd from an\n    35→          io.Writer via type assertion to *os.File. Required by pkg/format to suppress color\n    36→          output when stdout is redirected.\n    37→      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281): ls uses struct winsize to determine column count for its default multi-column layout and for the -w/--width flag default.'\n    38→      - R1.5: |\n    39→          Must provide func InstallSIGPIPEHandler() that installs a SIGPIPE handler causing the\n    40→          process to exit 0 when stdout or stderr is closed by a downstream consumer.\n    41→          Implementation: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of\n    42→          size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not\n    43→          use signal.Ignore so that deferred cleanup functions do not run on SIGPIPE (matching\n    44→          GNU behavior where SIGPIPE terminates silently).\n    45→      - R1.6: InstallSIGPIPEHandler must be safe to call multiple times; only one goroutine per invocation is started.\n    46→      - R1.7: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:1600-1620): ls installs a SIGPIPE handler via sigaction so that piping ls output to head does not produce a broken-pipe error message. du, find, and xargs have the same requirement.'\n    47→\n    48→  R2:\n    49→    title: Extended file metadata\n    50→    items:\n    51→      - R2.1: Must provide func Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) and func Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error). Stat follows symbolic links (equivalent to os.Stat); Lstat does not follow symbolic links (equivalent to os.Lstat). Both return a *FileInfo populated from syscall.Stat_t.\n    52→      - R2.2: |\n    53→          FileInfo must be defined as the following named struct in package sys:\n    54→\n    55→            type FileInfo struct {\n    56→                Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits (from syscall.Stat_t.Mode)\n    57→                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n    58→                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n    59→                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n    60→                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n    61→                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n    62→                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n    63→                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n    64→                Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n    65→                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n    66→                ModTime time.Time   // modification time (st_mtime / st_mtimespec)\n    67→                Info    os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo for os package compatibility\n    68→            }\n    69→      - R2.3: Must abstract the Darwin/Linux divergence in struct field names (st_mtimespec on Darwin vs st_mtim on Linux; st_blocks interpretation) so callers use a single API regardless of target platform.\n    70→      - R2.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:213, 371): ls needs Nlink, Uid, Gid, Rdev, Mode for -l format; Blocks for -s; Ino for -i.'\n    71→      - R2.5: 'Utility context — du (du.c:554): du needs Dev and Ino for hard-link deduplication, and Blocks for physical block-count accumulation.'\n    72→      - R2.6: 'Utility context — find (find/ftsfind.c:172): find predicates (-type, -size, -newer, -perm, -uid, -gid) require Mode, Size, ModTime, Uid, Gid, Nlink from the stat struct.'\n    73→\n    74→  R3:\n    75→    title: SIGWINCH handling\n    76→    items:\n    77→      - R3.1: |\n    78→          Must provide func OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that registers a SIGWINCH handler. When the terminal is resized, the handler calls TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width. If TerminalWidth() returns an error the callback is not invoked.\n    79→      - R3.2: Must support multiple calls to OnTerminalResize; each registered callback must be called on resize. Callbacks are called in registration order.\n    80→      - R3.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281, SIGWINCH registration): ls re-queries terminal width on SIGWINCH to re-format output when the terminal window is resized during a long listing.'\n    81→\n    82→non_goals:\n    83→  - pkg/sys does not wrap all of syscall or golang.org/x/sys; it exposes only the surfaces required by the current utility batch.\n    84→  - pkg/sys does not provide file-watching or inotify/kqueue interfaces.\n    85→  - pkg/sys does not handle SIGUSR1/SIGUSR2 (find's debug output); those are deferred to the find PRD.\n    86→  - pkg/sys does not provide process-group or terminal control (TIOCGPGRP, tcsetpgrp); those are deferred to the xargs PRD.\n    87→\n    88→acceptance_criteria:\n    89→  - \"AC1: TerminalWidth() returns a positive integer when called in a test with a pseudo-terminal (golang.org/x/term's test helpers or os.Pipe() as a TTY substitute is acceptable).\"\n    90→  - \"AC2: Stat() and Lstat() on a known file return FileInfo with correct Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, and Blocks values as confirmed by running stat(1) on the same file.\"\n    91→  - \"AC3: Lstat() on a symlink returns the symlink's own st_mode (type bits show symlink), not the target's mode.\"\n    92→  - \"AC4: InstallSIGPIPEHandler() causes a process writing to a broken pipe to exit 0 rather than printing a write error.\"\n    93→  - \"AC5: All pkg/sys code compiles on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 with no build errors and no cgo.\"\n    94→  - \"AC6: All pkg/sys code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    95→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5749d197-4955-4bd2-a1f1-74b483ba573f","uuid":"09bc02ab-2754-4f90-9948-f81575316001","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/185/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd002-sys.yaml","content":"id: prd002-sys\ntitle: \"pkg/sys: Syscall Abstractions\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Four utilities (ls, du, find, xargs) need OS-level capabilities that Go's standard\n  library does not expose in a portable, stable form: terminal-width queries, extended\n  file metadata (hard-link count, device and inode numbers, physical block counts),\n  and signal handling for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH. On Darwin and Linux these syscalls\n  diverge in struct layout (st_mtimespec vs st_mtim, st_blocks interpretation) and\n  available interfaces (TIOCGWINSZ ioctl). Without pkg/sys each cmd/ package would\n  import syscall or golang.org/x/sys directly, duplicating the Darwin/Linux divergence\n  handling and making it harder to test or audit the syscall surface.\n\n  pkg/sys provides a stable, tested interface for these three capability areas. Each\n  capability is driven by at least one concrete utility requirement derived from C source\n  analysis (see dependency-map.yaml).\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Provide a portable terminal-width query and SIGPIPE handler that work on Darwin and Linux without cgo.\n  - G2: Expose extended file metadata (st_nlink, st_uid, st_gid, st_rdev, st_blocks, st_ino, st_dev) not available from os.FileInfo.\n  - G3: Provide a SIGWINCH handler that calls a user-supplied callback when the terminal is resized.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Terminal width and TTY detection\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must provide func TerminalWidth() (int, error) that returns the current terminal column count by calling ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ) on stdout. Returns an error (not panic) when stdout is not a terminal or when ioctl fails.\n      - R1.2: Must use golang.org/x/sys/unix (the project's only external dependency) to perform the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl; must not shell out to tput or stty and must not import golang.org/x/term.\n      - R1.3: |\n          Must provide func IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool that returns true when the file\n          descriptor refers to a terminal, and false otherwise. Implementation: call\n          golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize(int(fd), unix.TIOCGWINSZ) and return\n          err == nil. Returns false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors.\n          The caller (pkg/format ColorEnabled) is responsible for extracting the fd from an\n          io.Writer via type assertion to *os.File. Required by pkg/format to suppress color\n          output when stdout is redirected.\n      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281): ls uses struct winsize to determine column count for its default multi-column layout and for the -w/--width flag default.'\n      - R1.5: |\n          Must provide func InstallSIGPIPEHandler() that installs a SIGPIPE handler causing the\n          process to exit 0 when stdout or stderr is closed by a downstream consumer.\n          Implementation: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of\n          size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not\n          use signal.Ignore so that deferred cleanup functions do not run on SIGPIPE (matching\n          GNU behavior where SIGPIPE terminates silently).\n      - R1.6: InstallSIGPIPEHandler must be safe to call multiple times; only one goroutine per invocation is started.\n      - R1.7: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:1600-1620): ls installs a SIGPIPE handler via sigaction so that piping ls output to head does not produce a broken-pipe error message. du, find, and xargs have the same requirement.'\n\n  R2:\n    title: Extended file metadata\n    items:\n      - R2.1: Must provide func Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) and func Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error). Stat follows symbolic links (equivalent to os.Stat); Lstat does not follow symbolic links (equivalent to os.Lstat). Both return a *FileInfo populated from syscall.Stat_t.\n      - R2.2: |\n          FileInfo must be defined as the following named struct in package sys:\n\n            type FileInfo struct {\n                Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits (from syscall.Stat_t.Mode)\n                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n                Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n                ModTime time.Time   // modification time (st_mtime / st_mtimespec)\n                Info    os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo for os package compatibility\n            }\n      - R2.3: Must abstract the Darwin/Linux divergence in struct field names (st_mtimespec on Darwin vs st_mtim on Linux; st_blocks interpretation) so callers use a single API regardless of target platform.\n      - R2.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:213, 371): ls needs Nlink, Uid, Gid, Rdev, Mode for -l format; Blocks for -s; Ino for -i.'\n      - R2.5: 'Utility context — du (du.c:554): du needs Dev and Ino for hard-link deduplication, and Blocks for physical block-count accumulation.'\n      - R2.6: 'Utility context — find (find/ftsfind.c:172): find predicates (-type, -size, -newer, -perm, -uid, -gid) require Mode, Size, ModTime, Uid, Gid, Nlink from the stat struct.'\n\n  R3:\n    title: SIGWINCH handling\n    items:\n      - R3.1: |\n          Must provide func OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that registers a SIGWINCH handler. When the terminal is resized, the handler calls TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width. If TerminalWidth() returns an error the callback is not invoked.\n      - R3.2: Must support multiple calls to OnTerminalResize; each registered callback must be called on resize. Callbacks are called in registration order.\n      - R3.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281, SIGWINCH registration): ls re-queries terminal width on SIGWINCH to re-format output when the terminal window is resized during a long listing.'\n\nnon_goals:\n  - pkg/sys does not wrap all of syscall or golang.org/x/sys; it exposes only the surfaces required by the current utility batch.\n  - pkg/sys does not provide file-watching or inotify/kqueue interfaces.\n  - pkg/sys does not handle SIGUSR1/SIGUSR2 (find's debug output); those are deferred to the find PRD.\n  - pkg/sys does not provide process-group or terminal control (TIOCGPGRP, tcsetpgrp); those are deferred to the xargs PRD.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: TerminalWidth() returns a positive integer when called in a test with a pseudo-terminal (golang.org/x/term's test helpers or os.Pipe() as a TTY substitute is acceptable).\"\n  - \"AC2: Stat() and Lstat() on a known file return FileInfo with correct Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, and Blocks values as confirmed by running stat(1) on the same file.\"\n  - \"AC3: Lstat() on a symlink returns the symlink's own st_mode (type bits show symlink), not the target's mode.\"\n  - \"AC4: InstallSIGPIPEHandler() causes a process writing to a broken pipe to exit 0 rather than printing a write error.\"\n  - \"AC5: All pkg/sys code compiles on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 with no build errors and no cgo.\"\n  - \"AC6: All pkg/sys code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":95,"startLine":1,"totalLines":95}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WP3r7QSnyfp5d6TdkReiLE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the requirements. Let me plan the implementation:\n\n1. **pkg/sys/doc.go** - Package-level documentation\n2. **pkg/sys/stat.go** - Platform-independent FileInfo struct, Stat/Lstat functions\n3. **pkg/sys/stat_darwin.go** - Darwin-specific fillFromStat (st_mtimespec, Blocks)\n4. **pkg/sys/stat_linux.go** - Linux-specific fillFromStat (st_mtim, Blocks)\n5. **pkg/sys/signal.go** - InstallSIGPIPEHandler, OnTerminalResize\n\n\n6. **pkg/sys/terminal.go** - TerminalWidth, IsTerminal\n\nI notice golang.org/x/sys isn't listed in go.mod yet, so I'll need to add it as a dependency before implementing the terminal functions.\n\nNow I'm planning out the structure: I'll write the package documentation first, then implement the core Stat and Lstat functions that work across platforms by calling the standard library and extracting the underlying syscall data. For Darwin specifically, I need to handle the st_mtimespec field to get the modification time, and I'll use build constraints to keep the platform-specific code separate.\n\nFor Linux, I'll do something similar but use st_mtim instead. Then I'll add the signal handling and terminal utilities—those don't need platform-specific code since the standard library already abstracts those differences away. The terminal functions will rely on golang.org/x/sys/unix to handle the cross-platform details. I'm realizing that since `golang.org/x/sys/unix` and the `syscall` package both work across darwin and linux, only the stat file actually needs platform-specific constraints due to differing field names in `Stat_t`. But I need to check whether the acceptance criteria requires all platform-specific code to be separated regardless.\n\nThe pattern I'm settling on is having a common stat.go that defines the public API and calls a platform-specific `fillFromStat` helper, with separate implementations in stat_darwin.go and stat_linux.go that handle the different `Stat_t` field layouts on each platform. Defining the platform-specific struct types...\n\nI'm noticing the field ordering and types differ across Darwin and Linux architectures, so I'll standardize everything to uint64 in the FileInfo struct and handle the conversions explicitly in each platform's implementation file. For the mode bits, I need to translate from the syscall representation into os.FileMode values.\n\nActually, I realize os.Stat already does this conversion properly, so I can just extract the Mode directly from the os.FileInfo that's returned. That's cleaner than trying to reconstruct it myself. The PRD shows I should store both the Mode and the underlying os.FileInfo for compatibility, so I'll pull the mode from there and structure the code with a platform-agnostic stat.go file that imports the necessary packages.\n\nSetting up the package structure... I'm defining a function that extracts the syscall stat structure from the file info, then populating a FileInfo struct with the basic metadata before delegating to a platform-specific helper to fill in the remaining fields. Now I'm implementing the Darwin-specific version that maps the stat_t fields like link count, user ID, and group ID into the FileInfo struct.\n\nContinuing with the Linux implementation...\n\nI'm noticing some important type differences between the Darwin and Linux syscall structures. On Darwin, `Dev` and `Rdev` are `int32`, but on Linux they're `uint64`. Similarly, `Nlink` varies—it's `uint16` on Darwin but `uint64` on Linux amd64 or `uint32` on arm64. And `Blksize` is `int32` on both Darwin and Linux arm64, which affects how I need to cast these values.\n\nI need to handle these platform-specific type conversions carefully to ensure safe casting across all architectures.\n\nNow I'm moving on to the signal handling code... Still writing signal handlers...\n\nActually, I'm realizing the terminal code needs platform-specific build constraints since it imports unix-specific packages. The task requires all platform-specific code to use _darwin.go or _linux.go suffixes with matching build constraints, so I should probably split this into separate files rather than having unix imports in a generic terminal.go file. since both darwin and linux share the same code, I could either use `//go:build darwin || linux` in terminal.go or rely on `golang.org/x/sys/unix` to handle platform differences internally. But that package won't compile on Windows, and since the project explicitly doesn't target Windows, I can skip build constraints. However, signal.go uses unix-specific signals like `SIGPIPE` and `SIGWINCH` that don't exist on Windows, so I should add build constraints to both files to be safe.\n\nWait, looking back at the requirements, AC5 only requires platform-specific code in suffixed files with matching constraints—code that works on both platforms doesn't necessarily need them.\n\nSince terminal.go uses golang.org/x/sys/unix which compiles identically on both darwin and linux, and signal.go uses syscall constants available on both, neither needs separate files. Only the stat code requires platform-specific files because the syscall.Stat_t struct differs between them.\n\nLet me finalize the design and start writing the code. I also need to add golang.org/x/sys as a dependency and run `go mod tidy` afterward. Looking at the current go.mod...\n\nActually, I notice the go.mod specifies Go 1.25.0, which is quite new, and golang.org/x/sys isn't listed as a dependency yet. I'll need to add it.\n\nRegarding tests—the task description lists specific files to create but doesn't include test files, and the acceptance criteria don't require them. Since I should only modify files explicitly listed, I won't create test files.\n\nFor the stat.go file, I need to import fmt for error messages. I'm also verifying the time field types across platforms—Darwin and Linux both use int64 for both seconds and nanoseconds in their respective stat structures, so time.Unix should work consistently.\n\nNow I'm checking the Blksize field types across architectures. On Linux arm64, Blksize is int32 while Nlink is uint32, and I need to verify the same pattern holds on amd64.\n\nComparing the struct definitions, I see that on amd64 both Blksize and Nlink are 64-bit types, so explicit casts like `uint64(st.Nlink)` and `int64(st.Blksize)` will safely handle the differences across platforms. Before writing the code, I should also consider that syscall.Stat_t is only available on Unix-like systems like Darwin, Linux, and FreeBSD, not on Windows.\n\nSince fillFromStat is only defined in the Darwin and Linux-specific files, stat.go will naturally fail to compile on unsupported platforms anyway. But to be explicit and clean, I'll add a build constraint to stat.go to restrict it to Darwin and Linux, matching the structure of the other platform-specific files.\n\nActually, reconsidering the task description—stat.go is labeled as \"platform-independent,\" which suggests it shouldn't have build constraints. The platform-specific implementations belong in the _darwin.go and _linux.go files. Let me keep stat.go, signal.go, and terminal.go without constraints and rely on the _darwin.go and _linux.go files to enforce the platform restrictions.\n\nWait, there's a subtlety here: if stat.go references syscall.Stat_t without a build constraint, it will compile on any Unix-like platform where that type exists, not just Darwin and Linux. On Windows, syscall.Stat_t doesn't exist, so the build would fail there anyway.\n\nThe linker will resolve fillFromStat at compile time—on Darwin it'll use the _darwin.go implementation, on Linux it'll use the _linux.go one, and on other platforms the link will fail, which is acceptable since we're not targeting them.\n\nI'm overcomplicating this. Let me just write the code. I also need to verify that terminal.go's import of golang.org/x/sys/unix is available in go.mod, so I'll run `go get golang.org/x/sys` after setting up the files.\n\nWriting the package files... Defining the FileInfo struct fields...\n\nNow I'm implementing the Stat and Lstat functions that wrap the standard library calls and convert the results into our extended FileInfo type, with Stat following symlinks and Lstat not following them.\n\nImplementing platform-specific file metadata extraction... I'm finishing up the function by setting the modification time from Darwin's timespec structure, then realizing I need to double-check the Go build constraint placement—it should come before the package declaration, though the exact position relative to copyright headers can vary. Let me look at how this project handles it in other files to stay consistent. I'm verifying the Go build constraint syntax—the `//go:build` directive needs to appear before the package declaration, and while a blank line between it and the package clause is conventional, the key requirement is just that it comes first in the file after any copyright headers.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the imports needed in stat.go: since the struct defines `ModTime time.Time`, the `time` package is required for the type definition itself, even though the actual value assignment happens in the platform-specific files through `fillFromStat`.\n\nFor the signal handler implementation, I need to clarify the requirement about goroutines—specifically whether \"only one goroutine per invocation\" means each call spawns a single goroutine, or if it's enforcing that only one goroutine total should be running across multiple calls to ensure the handler isn't duplicated. I think the key insight is that calling it multiple times just creates multiple goroutines, each listening for SIGPIPE independently—and that's perfectly fine since they all do the same thing. The straightforward implementation creates a channel and goroutine on each call, which satisfies both requirements: one goroutine per invocation, and safe to call multiple times without issues.\n\nNow moving on to the OnTerminalResize requirement, which needs to handle SIGWINCH signals and invoke a callback whenever the terminal dimensions change.\n\nSetting up a shared listener for SIGWINCH that manages multiple registered callbacks...\n\nImplementing the signal handler goroutine that retrieves the current terminal width and invokes all registered callbacks in order...\n\nWriting the terminal.go module with functions to get terminal width using ioctl syscalls and check if a file descriptor is a terminal...\n\nNow I need to verify that golang.org/x/sys is included in the go.mod file...\n\nit's not currently there, so I'll need to run `go get golang.org/x/sys` or `go mod tidy` after I create the files. Let me start by creating the pkg/sys/ directory and writing out all the files, then handle the dependency. Before I do that, I should double-check that the syscall package has SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH available...\n\nyes, both are defined on darwin and linux. For the signal types, I can pass syscall.SIGPIPE directly to signal.Notify since syscall.Signal implements os.Signal. Now I'll write out all the code.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2562,"cache_read_input_tokens":46414,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2562},"output_tokens":12,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5749d197-4955-4bd2-a1f1-74b483ba573f","uuid":"2ec23cab-0372-48a9-93db-802a8c50798c"}
